# Git Workflow: Branches, PRs, and Conventional Commits

For any new feature, fix, or scoped change — do not work directly on `main`.

## 1. Create a branch first

Before making changes:

```bash
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b <type>/<short-description>
```

**Branch naming** — lowercase, hyphenated, prefixed by change type:

| Type | Use for | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| `feat/` | New feature or capability | `feat/employee-invites` |
| `fix/` | Bug fix | `fix/login-redirect-loop` |
| `chore/` | Tooling, CI, deps, config | `chore/ci-and-branch-protection` |
| `refactor/` | Code change, no behavior change | `refactor/user-service-queries` |
| `docs/` | Documentation only | `docs/api-auth-guide` |
| `test/` | Tests only | `test/employee-service` |

## 2. Use conventional commits

Every commit message follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<optional scope>): <short summary>

[optional body]
```

**Types** (same as branch prefixes): `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `perf`, `ci`, `build`.

```bash
# ✅ GOOD
feat(employees): add bulk invite endpoint
fix(auth): redirect unauthenticated users to sign-in
chore(ci): add migration validation in PR pipeline

# ❌ BAD
Add employee invites
fixed bug
WIP
```

- Summary: imperative mood, no trailing period, ≤ 72 chars
- Scope: optional module or area (`employees`, `web`, `api`, `ci`)
- One logical change per commit when possible

## 3. Open a pull request

When the work is ready:

1. Push the branch: `git push -u origin HEAD`
2. Create a PR against `main` with `gh pr create`
3. PR title: same format as commit summary (e.g. `feat(employees): add bulk invite endpoint`)
4. PR body: brief summary, test plan, and link to any related issue

Do not merge to `main` locally. All changes land via PR review and CI.

## Agent behavior

- **Starting new work** → create the branch before editing files
- **Committing** → only when the user asks; use conventional commit format
- **Finishing** → push and open a PR when the user asks, or offer to do so once the task is complete
